@*
    Shared NON-destructive confirmation — "are you sure you want to leave, go
    back, or discard what you have typed?".

    Sibling of ConfirmDeleteDialog and deliberately the same anatomy, so the two
    read as one family: tinted header band with a ringed emblem, a body with
    room to breathe, and a tinted action bar where both choices carry equal hit
    area. The difference is temperature — amber, not red — because nothing here
    is removed from the gateway. The operator only loses what they typed into
    the step they are standing in, and a red dialog for that would cry wolf on
    the treatment reserved for deletes.

    Replaces DialogService.ShowMessageBox at the onboarding call sites. The
    message box rendered an untitled slab of body text with two identical-weight
    links, so "Continue" — the choice that throws work away — looked exactly as
    safe as "Stay here".

    Every string is a parameter: the flow asks several different questions
    ("Change source protocol?", "Exit setup?") and collapsing them into one
    shared sentence would lose the only information the operator needs.

    Geometry and colour live in wwwroot/css/site.css under .confirm-dialog —
    the class hooks below are the contract between the two files, so renaming
    one without the other silently unstyles the dialog.
*@

<MudDialog Class="confirm-dialog" ActionsClass="confirm-dialog__actions">
    <TitleContent>
        <div class="confirm-dialog__head">
            <span class="confirm-dialog__icon" aria-hidden="true">
                <MudIcon Icon="@Icon" />
            </span>
            <div>
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="confirm-dialog__title">@Title</MudText>
            </div>
        </div>
    </TitleContent>

    <DialogContent>
        <div class="confirm-dialog__body">
            <p class="confirm-dialog__message">@Message</p>

            @* The cost of continuing, stated plainly and LAST so it is the final
               thing read before the buttons. Boxed, because as trailing prose it
               is exactly what a hurried operator skims past. *@
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Consequence))
            {
                <div class="confirm-dialog__consequence" data-testid="confirm-dialog-consequence">
                    <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ReportProblem" Size="Size.Small" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <span>@Consequence</span>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </DialogContent>

    <DialogActions>
        <MudButton OnClick="Cancel" Class="confirm-dialog__cancel"
                   data-testid="confirm-dialog-cancel">
            @CancelLabel
        </MudButton>
        @* Warning, not Error: the confirm action is the one that costs the
           operator work, so it must lead the row — but it is not destructive. *@
        <MudButton Color="Color.Warning" Variant="Variant.Filled"
                   OnClick="Submit" Class="confirm-dialog__confirm"
                   data-testid="confirm-dialog-confirm">
            @ConfirmLabel
        </MudButton>
    </DialogActions>
</MudDialog>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Shared presentation for every non-destructive confirmation, so the call
    /// sites cannot drift apart. BackdropClick is disabled deliberately: a
    /// question about losing work has to be answered, not dismissed by a stray
    /// click outside the dialog. Escape still cancels, which is the safe choice.
    /// </summary>
    public static readonly DialogOptions Options = new()
    {
        // Small (~600px) rather than ExtraSmall (~444px): the consequence strip
        // wrapped to four cramped lines at the narrower size.
        MaxWidth = MaxWidth.Small,
        FullWidth = true,
        BackdropClick = false,
        CloseOnEscapeKey = true,
    };

    [CascadingParameter] private MudDialogInstance MudDialog { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Dialog heading, e.g. "Change source protocol?".</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Title { get; set; } = "Are you sure?";

    /// <summary>What the operator is about to do, in plain language.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Message { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>What they lose by continuing. Rendered as a highlighted strip; null hides it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Consequence { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Header emblem. Defaults to a back-arrow, which suits the "going back" cases.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Icon { get; set; } = Icons.Material.Filled.Undo;

    /// <summary>Confirm button text.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string ConfirmLabel { get; set; } = "Continue";

    /// <summary>Cancel button text.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string CancelLabel { get; set; } = "Stay here";

    private void Submit() => MudDialog.Close(DialogResult.Ok(true));

    private void Cancel() => MudDialog.Cancel();
}
